For "a taste of usage," the section should show what a Chevron application *looks like* without becoming a tutorial. A single minimal code block carrying the spine of a program is the right vehicle, surrounded by one framing sentence and one walk-through paragraph. What the code should make visible: the user owns `main`, the engine is constructed separately from the process, factory registration happens before commitment, and the process drives init and runtime. Anything beyond that belongs in tutorials.

Draft:

> A Chevron application is bootstrapped from the user's own `main` — Chevron supplies the structure, not the entry point.
>
> ```cpp
> #include <chevron/process/app_process.hpp>
> #include <chevron/wx/engine.hpp>
>
> int main(int argc, char** argv) {
>     chevron::process::AppProcess app;
>
>     auto engine = std::make_unique<chevron::wx::Engine>();
>     engine->windowing().registerFactory(makeMainWindowFactory());
>
>     app.commitEngine(std::move(engine));
>     app.initialize();
>     return app.startRuntime();
> }
> ```
>
> The user constructs an `AppProcess`, builds the concrete engine for their chosen framework, registers window factories through the engine's windowing surface, commits the engine to the process, and runs. Everything else — subsidiary windows, runtime dispatches, infrastructure access — happens from inside the application after the mainloop is running.

A few honest placeholders. Method names (`commitEngine`, `initialize`, `startRuntime`) come from the terminology in your class docs but the final names are your call. `makeMainWindowFactory()` stands in for whatever the user supplies. The sketch shows the happy path only — init likely returns a status in real code, but this section is about shape, not error handling.

